t scene and it  morphs into cable car  which is now we're in San Francisco  right that's great I love that I love  that so you're using audio as your  transition instead of visual and if it's  effective and it's doing doing all that  then it's it brings the audience in and  they or like I like to say the sound  sucks you into the next scene  right and we did it throughout the whole  movie you know and subliminal level and  um  um and it you know it worked you know  really well uh in in that movie and um  which brings me to you know I think  there was a question about are there  things that we create that are not in  the script you know to to help forward  the movie and and as seeing once again  in the jury Luck Club  where this the scene is with um  uh the same woman uh ying ying and her  daughter  um living in this crooked house and so  she's walking around and her whole thing  is you know I'm like a tiger on a prowl  um  so Wayne comes into my Cutting Room and  and he had this look on his face like oh  what's going on and he goes well I've  been accused of this and I guess I'm  I'll be accused of it again I go what is  that he goes being abstract I go well  let's talk about it I go what's on your  mind and he goes well  I'm having  I'm thinking I I'm not quite sure how we  bridge this  this scene where she's walking around  and it feels like it could be too long  and um I said well what do you think  about this you know you know because  editors and directors we we actually  spend a lot of time talking a lot of  people when they walk by The Cutting  Room they go oh they're just talking  they're not working but we get a lot of  work done just talking and and delving  into things and I said to him I go or  here's a woman that kills her baby you  know it's 20 years later I said she's  still gonna be thinking about that  almost daily I said what have we just  cut a bunch of stuff that that builds up  to that moment and then  um he had shot uh the vas breaking you  know and it kind of cues  um  the the one woman and her husband to  look up the noise cues them Something's  Happened upstairs and so that's how that  Montage came about that you see the baby  you see the the bad husband and the  watermelon and we accentuated all those  sounds you know and so at the end is  when the Voz breaks and once again it  echoes through the house and that cues  the daughter to come up and to get the  final lesson  that's that's great I love that and that  wasn't in the script at all so I I  really that was fun that was a fun one  to work on create that montage  that's true  with with the material you have and and  really going above and beyond and from  what you're describing obviously your  career has been so busy and jam-packed  how did you balance work and family and  and coming back to work after some time  off with your children how did you  balance all that and how did you manage  to come back  well I decided to take like a Hiatus  from working as  um a sound editor actually because when  I work with Bob I would like on three  women for instance  um he put me in that movie  um and and how I got that job was once  again I'm walking down the hall and he  looks at me and goes you're Doris and  you're going to Palm Springs I go okay I  don't know what that means  and  but the backstory to that there's always  a back story here is that I was living  with uh Scotty Bush now we were sharing  an apartment and one morning you know  the phone rings at like six o'clock in  the morning and  um she's on the phone for like at least  two hours you know I get up shower I'd  go sit down have coffee with her and she  goes well that was Bob I go oh really  what what did he want he goes well he  had a dream  we're doing a movie called three women  so that was the reference  which is crazy right love it  so so  that's when he pointed to me like okay  you're Doris who was one of the the spa  workers that turned out doors in one of  the spa workers and so  um I 