d who's looking for an  assistant I go really and she said yeah  can I give him your name I said sure and  he calls me and interviews me over the  phone and I get hired by Danny Green  so I go off and I work with Danny and  um you know God Rest his soul Danny's  passed away but he became a great mentor  to me and he was super old school  because back in the day editors loved to  go have lunch in a place where they had  a bar  right yep  and so I  the soup to go  right  Niche let's go have a lunch at a shishi  place that's what he would call it and  so we'd go to Musso and Frank's we'd go  oh my God we'd go all over Hollywood and  but I noticed that when we'd come back  he'd sort of sit down at the couch and  then he'd have the trades and then next  thing you know he was asleep  so this happened like  like the whole week and before the end  of the week like the like Wednesday or  something like that I go great so what  am I gonna do read the script again  so finally  one day I knew he was just about ready  to doze off I go listen I go hey Danny  would you mind if I work on on a scene  um and he goes knock yourself out  darling and off off he'd go and so uh so  I'd work on the scene  and and the only reason I asked him that  was because we were working in tandem so  he'd be working on the bigger machine I  work on the smaller machine and he'd go  okay at this point I need a close-up on  this actor okay off I go I look cut it  hand it to him he you know splice it in  this would go on he never said to me  goes why'd you pick that he just put it  in and then we'd play it so I kind of  knew that we had we were in sync  so I finally said to him I go okay Hey  listen Danny well you know you're you're  relaxing uh would you mind if I work on  a scene and he goes oh knock yourself  out Marlin  relaxing relaxing anyways so I'd work on  a scene at about five o'clock I'd go tap  tap tap hey Danny  um before we go home would you mind  looking at what I did today and you go  and g